.TH GPIO-SLOPPY-LOGIC-ANALYZER 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer \- capture GPIO samples with the kernel's sloppy logic analyzer
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
.RB [ \-c
.IR cpu ]
.RB [ \-d
.IR microseconds " | " \c
.B \-n
.IR samples ]
.RB [ \-s
.IR frequency ]
.RB [ \-t
.IR trigger ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR instance ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR debugfs\-dir ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output\-dir ]
.br
.B \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
.RB [ \-k
.IR debugfs\-dir ]
.B \-l
.br
.B \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer \-h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
is a shell script that drives the kernel's GPIO sloppy logic analyzer
driver.
The driver samples up to eight GPIO lines, called probes, by polling them
from a CPU with local interrupts and preemption disabled for the whole
capture.
Latencies, non-deterministic code paths and non-maskable interrupts affect
the result, which is why the analyzer is called sloppy.
The script isolates a CPU for the sampling, configures one analyzer
instance through debugfs, and saves the samples as a sigrok session file.
.PP
The script:
.IP 1. 4
checks that
.BR taskset (1)
and
.BR zip (1)
are available;
.IP 2. 4
looks in
.I /proc/self/mounts
for a mounted cgroup version 1 hierarchy with the
.B cpuset
controller, and if there is none, creates
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
if it is missing and mounts such a hierarchy there;
.IP 3. 4
selects the analyzer instance
(see
.BR \-i );
.IP 4. 4
isolates a CPU, if
.B \-c
is given or no CPU has been isolated yet (see
.BR "CPU isolation" );
.IP 5. 4
writes the sampling period, the number of samples and any trigger to the
instance's debugfs files;
.IP 6. 4
prints the instance, number of samples, frequency, trigger and CPU it will
use;
.IP 7. 4
starts the capture in a background process and exits.
.PP
The background process writes to the instance's
.I capture
file from a process bound to the isolated CPU with
.BR taskset (1).
The kernel samples on that CPU, waiting first for the trigger, if one was
given; the write returns when the capture has finished.
The process then saves the result file and prints
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
Logic analyzer done. Saved '\fIfile\fP'
Max sample frequency this time: \fIF\fPHz.
.EE
.in
.PP
where the frequency is derived from the time the driver measured for one
read of all probes.
If the capture fails, it prints
\(dqCapture error! Check kernel log\(dq
instead.
No result file exists until the background process has finished.
.SS CPU isolation
To isolate CPU
.IR n ,
the script:
.IP \(bu 2
creates the cpuset
.I gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
in the cpuset hierarchy if it does not exist;
if that cpuset already contains a different CPU, the script fails;
if it already contains CPU
.IR n ,
nothing further is done;
.IP \(bu 2
writes
.I n
to its
.IR cpuset.cpus ,
1 to its
.I cpuset.cpu_exclusive
and 0 to its
.IR cpuset.mems ;
.IP \(bu 2
clears CPU
.I n
from the mask in
.I /proc/irq/default_smp_affinity
and writes the result to every file under
.I /proc/irq
whose name ends in
.I smp_affinity
and to every file named
.I cpumask
under
.IR /sys/devices/virtual/workqueue ,
ignoring errors;
.IP \(bu 2
for each process listed by
.B ps \-o pid
whose CPU affinity equals the original default mask,
sets its affinity to the new mask with
.BR taskset (1);
.IP \(bu 2
writes 1 to
.IR /sys/module/rcupdate/parameters/rcu_cpu_stall_suppress ,
suppressing RCU CPU stall warnings;
.IP \(bu 2
sets the CPU's cpufreq scaling governor to
.B performance
if its
.I scaling_governor
file is writable.
.PP
The script never undoes these changes.
Later runs find the existing cpuset and reuse the CPU isolated in it.
The CPU used for sampling is read from the cpuset's
.I cpuset.effective_cpus
file.
.SS Result file
The result is written to
.IR output\-dir / instance \- seconds .sr,
where
.I seconds
is the time the capture finished, in seconds since the Epoch.
It is a sigrok session file, a zip archive readable by PulseView and
sigrok\-cli from the sigrok project, containing:
.TP
.I version
The session format version, 1.
.TP
.I metadata
The capture description:
the number of probes,
the sample rate (the requested
.IR frequency ),
one byte per sample,
and the probe names from the driver.
.TP
.I logic\-1\-1
The raw samples, one byte per sample, bit 0 holding probe 1.
.SH OPTIONS
Long options may be abbreviated, and may also be given with a single
leading dash, as
.BR getopt (1)
with
.B \-a
allows.
.PP
The
.IR microseconds ,
.IR samples ,
and
.I frequency
arguments are integers that may carry the suffixes
.BR K ,
.BR M ,
.BR G ,
and
.BR T ,
in either case, for 10^3, 10^6, 10^9 and 10^12;
several may be combined, so
.B 1M500K
is 1500000.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-cpu " \fIcpu\fP"
Isolate CPU
.I cpu
for sampling, as described under
.BR "CPU isolation" .
This is needed only once.
Without this option, CPU 1 is isolated if no CPU has been isolated yet.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-duration\-us " \fImicroseconds\fP"
Set the duration of the capture in microseconds.
The number of samples is computed as
.IR frequency " * " microseconds " / 1000000"
and overrides
.BR \-n .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a help message and exit.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-instance " \fIinstance\fP"
Use the analyzer instance named
.IR instance ,
a subdirectory of the analyzer's debugfs directory.
The default is the first instance directory found.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-kernel\-debug\-dir " \fIdebugfs\-dir\fP"
The directory where debugfs is mounted.
The default is
.IR /sys/kernel/debug .
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-list\-instances
Print the names of the available analyzer instances, one per line,
and exit.
The script first performs steps 1 and 2 above, so this may mount the
cpuset hierarchy.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-num_samples " \fIsamples\fP"
Set the number of samples to acquire.
The default is 250000.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output\-dir " \fIoutput\-dir\fP"
Write the result file to
.IR output\-dir ,
which must exist.
The default is the current working directory.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-sample_freq " \fIfrequency\fP"
Sample at
.I frequency
hertz.
The default is 1000000.
The script writes a sampling period of 1000000000 /
.I frequency
nanoseconds, rounded down, to the driver.
If that period is shorter than the time the driver measures for one read of
all probes, or is 0, the capture fails.
A frequency of 0 is rejected.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trigger " \fItrigger\fP"
Wait for
.I trigger
before sampling.
.I trigger
is one or more steps separated by commas, and each step is one or more
conditions separated by
.BR + .
A condition is a probe number from 1 to 8 followed by a level:
.BR L ,
low;
.BR H ,
high;
.BR R ,
rising edge;
or
.BR F ,
falling edge;
in either case.
All conditions of a step must be met at the same time,
and the steps must be met in order.
The driver detects an edge by first waiting for the level before it
and then for the level after it, so for a step with an edge condition
it waits for two states in turn.
The state that completes the trigger is the first sample recorded.
The trigger applies to the next capture only; the driver then discards it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The help message or the instance list was printed,
or the capture was started.
The outcome of the background capture is not reflected in the exit status.
.TP
.B 1
The command line could not be parsed, a required command is missing,
or a check failed; a message is printed.
.PP
The script runs under
.BR "sh \-e" ,
so it also exits, with that command's status, when any other command it
runs fails.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /sys/kernel/debug/gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer/
The analyzer's debugfs directory, with one subdirectory per instance.
The prefix changes with
.BR \-k .
.TP
.IR \&.../ instance /
Instance files used by the script:
.I delay_ns
(sampling period in nanoseconds),
.I buf_size
(number of samples),
.I trigger
(trigger data),
.I capture
(starts a capture),
.I sample_data
(the samples),
.I meta_data
(probe names) and
.I delay_ns_acquisition
(measured time for one read of all probes).
.TP
.I /proc/self/mounts
Searched for a mounted cpuset hierarchy.
.TP
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
Mount point used when no cpuset hierarchy is mounted.
.TP
.IR cpuset\-mount /gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer/
The cpuset holding the isolated CPU, with the files
.IR cpuset.cpus ,
.IR cpuset.cpu_exclusive ,
.I cpuset.mems
and
.IR cpuset.effective_cpus .
If the hierarchy is mounted with the
.B noprefix
option, the files have no
.I cpuset.\&
prefix.
.TP
.I /proc/irq/default_smp_affinity
Default interrupt affinity mask.
It is read, and then overwritten along with the
.I smp_affinity
files of the individual interrupts under
.IR /proc/irq .
.TP
.I /sys/devices/virtual/workqueue/
Searched for workqueue
.I cpumask
files.
.TP
.I /sys/module/rcupdate/parameters/rcu_cpu_stall_suppress
RCU CPU stall warning suppression.
.TP
.IR /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu N /cpufreq/scaling_governor
Scaling governor of the isolated CPU.
.SH NOTES
The kernel must be built with
.BR \%CONFIG_GPIO_SLOPPY_LOGIC_ANALYZER ,
which depends on
.BR \%CONFIG_EXPERT ,
.B \%CONFIG_CPUSETS
and
.BR \%CONFIG_DEBUG_FS ,
and the probes must be described to the kernel, for example by a
Device Tree node compatible with
.B \%gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
with
.I probe\-gpios
and
.I probe\-names
properties.
The driver supports at most 8 probes and rejects GPIOs whose access may
sleep.
debugfs must be mounted, at
.I /sys/kernel/debug
or at the directory given with
.BR \-k .
.PP
The script uses the cgroup version 1 cpuset interface,
which requires
.BR \%CONFIG_CPUSETS_V1 .
When no cpuset hierarchy is mounted, the script mounts one on
.I /sys/fs/cgroup
without checking whether something is already mounted there.
If the system uses cpusets for other purposes, check that the script does
not interfere with them.
.PP
The script mounts a filesystem, writes kernel settings under
.I /proc
and
.IR /sys ,
uses debugfs and changes the CPU affinity of other processes;
it must be run with the privileges these operations require.
.PP
Besides
.BR taskset (1)
and
.BR zip (1),
the script uses
.BR awk (1),
.BR sed (1),
.BR find (1),
.BR ps (1),
.BR mount (8),
.BR mktemp (1),
and a
.BR getopt (1)
that supports long options.
.SH EXAMPLES
Take 250000 samples at 1 MHz from the first instance, isolating CPU 1 if
no CPU has been isolated yet:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer
.EE
.in
.PP
Sample an I2C bus, with SCL on probe 1 and SDA on probe 2, at 1.5 MHz for
15 ms, starting at a start condition (SDA falling while SCL is high):
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer \-s 1500000 \-t 1H+2F \-d 15000
.EE
.in
.PP
Sample for 50 microseconds at 2 MHz, starting at a falling edge on probe 2:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer \-d 50 \-s 2M \-t 2F
.EE
.in
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR getopt (1),
.BR taskset (1),
.BR zip (1),
.BR cpuset (7),
.BR \%gpio\-event\-mon (8),
.BR \%gpio\-watch (8)
.PP
Setting up the analyzer is described in the Linux kernel source in
.PP
.I \%Documentation/dev\-tools/gpio\-sloppy\-logic\-analyzer.rst
